[[main()]]

The program that *uses* a [[Class]] rather than defining one. It `#include`s the
[[Header File]], creates objects, calls the public member functions, and never sees the
[[Implementation]].

```c++
#include "intstack.h"
int main() {
	IntStack s;
	s.push(4);
	int x;
	s.pop(x);
	return 0;
}
```

Being a client is a restriction you accept on purpose: you can only use what the
[[Interface]] offers, so you cannot come to depend on anything that might change. The other
side of that bargain is that when the implementation is rewritten, your code still works.

The word is relative, not absolute. `main.cpp` is the client of `intstack.h`, which is itself
a client of `<iostream>`. Same relationship, different layer. See [[Interface Module]] for how
the files fit together, and [[Coupling and Cohesion]] in
[[INTRODUCTION TO SOFTWARE ENGINEERING]] for why the arrangement is worth the trouble.
